{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Clapham1969-PBS-b-HL-m4",
  "Source": "P. B. Clapham, M. J. Downs, and R. J. King, \"Some applications of thin films to polarization devices,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 1965-1974 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.001965 - Design formula p. 1967 (Eq. (1), (3)); indices from Table I footnote (p. 1968); quarter waves at operating angle p. 1969; lambda0 = 546 nm p. 1969",
  "Notes": "Cube polarizing beam splitter (MacNeille type), design (HL)^4, high Rs and zero Rp at 45 deg in glass\nSpecification: Rs as high as possible and Rp = 0 at 546 nm, 45 deg in the glass.\nReported: Table I (p. 1968), column 'HL' (b), m = 4: Rs = 100.0 % at 546 nm, 45 deg in glass, computed from Eq. (3); Rp = 0 for all m. Built and measured (p. 1969): eight-layer Bi2O3/MgF2 polarizer of design (HL)^4 in EDF glass: polarizing efficiencies > 99 % over 425-675 nm (reflected) and 525-675 nm (transmitted); 99.8 % in both beams at the design wavelength 546 nm; total absorption loss in films and glass (12-mm cube) averages 10 % for the two polarizations; single-layer AR coatings on the cube faces reduced face reflection to ~1 % per surface.\nNotes: Table I combination (b): nG = 1.70 (extra dense flint), nL = 1.385 (MgF2), nH = 2.44 (reactively sputtered Bi2O3). Polarizing beam-splitter cube: the multilayer sits on the 45-deg diagonal of a cemented glass cube, light in the glass at 45 deg, which by Eq. (1) is the Brewster angle at every internal film interface (computed theta_L = 60.22 deg, theta_H = 29.52 deg). Every layer is a quarter wave of OPTICAL thickness AT THE OPERATING ANGLE at lambda0 = 546 nm (paper p. 1969: 'optical thickness 0.25 lambda0 under conditions of operation'), so thicknesses are given in physical nm computed as d = 546/(4 n cos theta): L = 198.426 nm, H = 64.285 nm (= 2.0133 and 1.1491 normal-incidence QWOT at 546 nm). Incident and exit media are the same glass (constant index from the paper); the parallel-sided cement layer (n ~ 1.58) is ignored, as in the paper's Table I computation. Lossless materials as in Table I. Polarizing efficiency (footnote p. 1966) = (I1 - I2)/(I1 + I2): reflected (Rs - Rp)/(Rs + Rp), transmitted (Tp - Ts)/(Tp + Ts). (HL)^4 = 8 layers, H first next to the incident glass as written in Table I (the text also writes (LH)^m; for a lossless stack between identical glasses Rs is the same either way). Check by the closed form Eq. (3) (squared) gives Rs = 99.95 % (Table I prints 100.0 %). No total thickness is printed. Bi2O3 is absorbing (k = 0.02 at 633 nm, larger at shorter wavelengths, p. 1968), so the measured cube has losses; Table I is lossless.\nLayers from Design formula p. 1967 (Eq. (1), (3)); indices from Table I footnote (p. 1968); quarter waves at operating angle p. 1969; lambda0 = 546 nm p. 1969; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours Rs 100 %, Rp 4.9e-4 at 546 nm, 45 deg in glass; paper Rs 100.0 %, Rp 0 (Table I).",
